<h3>Flex Fitness vs Cubii JR2</h3>
<p>This is the comparison most shoppers end up making. Both are manual seated ellipticals with a smooth oval stride, so the basic experience is similar. The differences show up after a few weeks of daily use.</p>
<p>The Cubii JR2 uses a magnetic flywheel that owners consistently describe as near-silent, with a clearer resistance range and a display that tracks strides and calories. It is heavier, which helps it stay put. Cubii also has an established support and replacement-parts setup. The Flex Fitness is simpler and usually cheaper, which is fine for gentle movement but leaves less room to grow. If you expect to use the machine every workday for years, the JR2 is the better investment. If you want to test the habit first, the Flex Fitness is a lower-risk start.</p>
<h3>Which one should you pick?</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Gentle movement on a budget:</strong> the Flex Fitness or the [[amazon:sunny-sf-e3872|Sunny Health &amp; Fitness under desk elliptical]], which owners rate for value.</li>
<li><strong>Quietest and smoothest:</strong> the Cubii JR2, our best overall seated elliptical.</li>
<li><strong>Low desk or tall user:</strong> the DeskCycle Ellipse, whose low pedal height clears more desks. See our <a href="/deskcycle-ellipse-review/">DeskCycle Ellipse review</a>.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Realistic Results: What to Expect</h2>
<p>Light seated pedaling sits at roughly 2.0 METs on the Compendium of Physical Activities, compared with about 1.3 for sitting still. For most people that works out to somewhere around 100 to 200 calories an hour at a light to moderate pace, depending on body weight and effort.</p>
<p>A realistic routine looks like three or four 15 to 20 minute blocks across the day: one during morning email, one after lunch when energy dips, and one or two during afternoon calls. That is an hour of extra movement without blocking any time on your calendar.</p>
<p>That will not transform your body on its own, but an hour or two spread across a workday adds up across a month. It also counts toward the CDC and WHO guideline of 150 minutes of moderate activity a week if you pedal with real effort. For the full picture, read <a href="/can-you-lose-weight-with-under-desk-elliptical/">can you lose weight with an under desk elliptical</a>.</p>

<h2>Setup Tips for Better Sessions</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Sit slightly back.</strong> Push the chair back a few inches so your knees stay below the desk edge at the top of the stride.</li>
<li><strong>Start low.</strong> Ten to fifteen minutes at light resistance is plenty for the first week. Build from there.</li>
<li><strong>Pedal in blocks.</strong> Short sessions every half hour are easier to keep up than one long session. The <a href="/tools/desk-pedal-timer/">desk pedal timer</a> will prompt you.</li>
<li><strong>Protect the floor.</strong> Put a [[amazon:cubii-mat|non-slip mat]] under the machine on hardwood or tile.</li>
</ol>
<p>Keep your back against the chair, feet flat on the pedals and knees tracking straight ahead. If one knee drifts outward, slow down and lower the resistance until the motion feels even on both sides.</p>

<h2>Our Verdict on the Flex Fitness Under Desk Elliptical</h2>
<p>The Flex Fitness under desk elliptical does what its "seated leg energizer" label promises: easy, quiet leg movement for people who sit a lot. It is a sensible buy for seniors and light users who value simplicity over performance, as long as you check the seller's warranty and return terms.</p>
